b-mcp-win-x64.zip\"},{\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/releases/assets/683608576\",\"id\":683608576,\"node_id\":\"RA_kwDOTDJ7sc4ovwoA\",\"name\":\"SHA256SUMS\",\"label\":\"\",\"uploader\":{\"login\":\"github-actions[bot]\",\"id\":41898282,\"node_id\":\"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=\",\"avatar_url\":\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4\",\"gravatar_id\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D\",\"html_url\":\"https://github.com/apps/github-actions\",\"followers_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers\",\"following_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}\",\"gists_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}\",\"starred_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}\",\"subscriptions_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions\",\"organizations_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs\",\"repos_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos\",\"events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}\",\"received_events_url\":\"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events\",\"type\":\"Bot\",\"user_view_type\":\"public\",\"site_admin\":false},\"content_type\":\"application/octet-stream\",\"state\":\"uploaded\",\"size\":1073,\"digest\":\"sha256:2b64891fad0e5aa8b904328ea285803b066eaab9279e8396e553596254aa38ac\",\"download_count\":2,\"created_at\":\"2026-10-28T02:47:11Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-10-28T02:47:11Z\",\"browser_download_url\":\"https://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/releases/download/v2.7.0/SHA256SUMS\"}],\"tarball_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/tarball/v2.7.0\",\"zipball_url\":\"https://api.github.com/repos/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/zipball/v2.7.0\",\"body\":\"## Gramps Web MCP 2.7.0\\n\\nUndo, confirmation dialogs and a server binary for every platform. `undo_change` reverts one transaction from the edit history, deletions included. In clients that support MCP elicitation, `delete_object` and `undo_change` ask the user, not the model, before they change the tree. Each release now carries the self-contained server for five platforms, so MCP clients other than Claude Desktop can run it without Docker or .NET, and every release file and image has a build provenance attestation.\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **`undo_change`** — Undoes one transaction listed by `get_recent_changes`, by its `[transaction: N]` number. Records it added are deleted, records it updated get their earlier version back, and records it deleted are restored with the same handles and Gramps IDs, all as one new all-or-nothing transaction that `undo_change` can revert in turn. `dryRun` lists the changes without writing.\\n  - Any conflict refuses the undo with nothing changed and names the records: one changed again after the transaction (Gramps Web's own check), one to delete that other records now link to, or an earlier version that links a record which no longer exists. Undo the later transactions first, newest first, or pass `force=true` after a separate confirmation.\\n  - Gramps Web before API 3.23 restores deleted records only with `force=true`; the refusal says so when nothing else conflicts. A background undo (Gramps Web with a task queue) is followed for up to 45 seconds and otherwise reported as `pending`.\\n- **Confirmation dialogs** — In clients that support [MCP elicitation](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/client/elicitation), `delete_object` and `undo_change` ask the user before they change the tree. The dialog names the record to delete and, with `force=true`, the records that will keep a link to it, or lists what the undo will delete and restore and the conflicts it will override. Only a ticked box goes ahead; declining or closing the dialog returns `action: declined` or `action: cancelled` with nothing changed. The record, or the transaction's conflicts, are checked again after the answer, and a change made while the user was deciding refuses the call. Dry runs and refusals do not ask.\\n- **`GRAMPS_CONFIRMATION`** — `when-supported` (default) asks clients that support elicitation and runs as before in the others, `required` refuses deletes and undos in clients that cannot ask (`action: not_confirmed`), `off` never asks. Stateless Streamable HTTP cannot ask; `MCP_STATELESS=false` enables the dialog over HTTP. The server logs once per client whether it asks.\\n- **Permission metadata** — `delete_object` and `undo_change` carry the tool metadata `anthropic/requiresUserInteraction`, so clients that honour it, such as Claude Code, ask their own permission before the call.\\n- **Server binaries** — Each release also carries the self-contained server for `osx-arm64`, `osx-x64`, `win-x64`, `linux-x64` and `linux-arm64` as `gramps-web-mcp-<rid>.tar.gz` (`.zip` for Windows), the same single file the MCPB bundles hold, so MCP clients other than Claude Desktop can start it over stdio without Docker or .NET. The names carry no version, so `releases/latest/download/<name>` always fetches the newest one, and `SHA256SUMS` lists the checksums of the release files. See [Over stdio with the release binary](https://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp#over-stdio-with-the-release-binary).\\n- **Build provenance attestations** — Every file attached to the release (the MCPB bundles, the binary archives and `SHA256SUMS`) and every image the workflow pushes has a signed record of the repository, workflow and commit that built it. Check a download or an image with the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/) (with Docker Desktop installed, start it first: `gh` asks it for registry credentials):\\n\\n  ```bash\\n  gh attestation verify gramps-web-mcp-osx-arm64.tar.gz --repo Scormave/gramps-web-mcp\\n  gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/scormave/gramps-web-mcp:2.7.0 --repo Scormave/gramps-web-mcp\\n  ```\\n\\n  Releases up to 2.6.0 and their images have none.\\n- **Release checks** — A `v*` tag that does not match the version in the project file and a dated changelog section publishes nothing. Before the image is pushed and before the bundles are attached to a release, a smoke test starts the built server against a stand-in for Gramps Web and runs MCP over it; for the bundles it passes the default settings as Claude Desktop does, which would have caught the 2.5.0 `${HOME}` bug. Every Monday a workflow scans the published images with Trivy for HIGH and CRITICAL vulnerabilities that have a fix. A test keeps the documentation in step with the registered tools, resources and prompts.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- **`delete_object`** no longer describes a deletion as permanent: `undo_change` with the deletion's transaction restores the record. The server instructions and the `get_recent_changes` description say when to use `undo_change` and where its transaction number comes from.\\n- **Client setup** — The README shows how to connect Claude Code, VS Code, Cursor, Codex, Antigravity CLI and Open WebUI: over HTTP to a running server with the API key, or over stdio with the client starting the Docker image. It replaces the `dotnet run` example, which needed a source checkout and the .NET SDK. Install buttons add the Docker entry to VS Code, VS Code Insiders and Cursor; VS Code asks for the Gramps Web address, user name, password and tree ID on the first start and keeps the password as a secret; Cursor adds the entry with placeholders to replace.\\n- **What to ask** — The README lists twelve example requests in plain words, from how two people are related to undoing the last edit, each with the tools a typical run uses and what comes back.\\n- **Dependencies** — Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting and Microsoft.Extensions.Http 10.0.13. The integration tests run against Gramps Web 26.10.2.\\n\\n### Upgrade from 2.6.0\\n\\nNo settings to change. With the defaults, clients that support MCP elicitation now ask before deletes and undos, and the others run them as before.\\n\\n**Claude Desktop.** Install the new MCPB bundle and check that the extension is enabled afterwards.\\n\\n**Claude Code and other clients that honour `anthropic/requiresUserInteraction`** may now ask their own permission before `delete_object` and `undo_change`. With a confirmation dialog as well, the user may confirm twice; `GRAMPS_CONFIRMATION=off` keeps only the client's prompt.\\n\\n**Docker over HTTP.** The default stateless HTTP cannot show the dialog. Set `MCP_STATELESS=false` to confirm deletes and undos over HTTP. With `GRAMPS_CONFIRMATION=required`, clients that cannot ask get every delete and undo refused.\\n\\n**Building from source.** `scripts/pack-mcpb.sh` is now `scripts/pack-release.sh` and packs the binary archive too; the MCPB workflow is now `release-assets.yml`.\\n\\n### Install\\n\\n**Docker**\\n\\n```bash\\ndocker pull ghcr.io/scormave/gramps-web-mcp:2.7.0\\n```\\n\\n**Claude Desktop** — Download the MCPB bundle for your platform from the assets below (`osx-arm64`, `osx-x64`, `win-x64`, `linux-x64`, or `linux-arm64`).\\n\\n**Other MCP clients without Docker** — Download the archive for your platform from the assets below and follow [Over stdio with the release binary](https://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp#over-stdio-with-the-release-binary).\\n\\n**From source** — Requires the .NET 10 SDK. Docker images, self-contained MCPB bundles and the binary archives include the runtime. A Release build needs a Six Labors license key, which ImageSharp 4 checks at compile time. The repository includes a free community key for the project's assemblies, so no setup is needed.\\n\\n### Full changelog\\n\\nhttps://github.com/Scormave/gramps-web-mcp/compare/v2.6.0...v2.7.0\\n\"}"
